  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 1214 AM MST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nOur next approaching storm system is visible on satellite imagery \nmoving across the Great Basin tonight. Current METAR observations \nshow snow showers have begun to move into the northern mountains, \nwhere southwesterly flow will favor the Park Range. 3-6 inches \nare forecast between today and Saturday, with locally higher \namounts possible at the highest elevations. As this shortwave approaches,\nsouthwesterly flow will steadily increase as the night progresses\nand an 80-90kt 500mb jet moves over Colorado. While there will be\nenough moisture for light mountain snow showers today, the main \nweather impacts will be with gusty winds and elevated to near- \ncritical fire weather conditions developing across portions of the\nplains and Palmer Divide this afternoon. Gusty southwesterly \nwinds mixed with above-normal temperatures are expected to drop \nrelative humidity (RH) values down to 17%-22% across the plains, \nwhere 30-40mph winds are expected to develop by late morning. \nThere isn't expected to be enough of an overlap with the lowest RH\nand gusty winds to warrant any fire weather highlights, but \nelevated fire weather conditions can be expected across much of \nthe lower elevations this afternoon. Additionally, with the jet \noverhead, expect strong winds gusting up to 65-70mph at times over\nour high mountain peaks, but gusts should generally be 40-50mph \nover the mountains, with some 30-40mph gusts making their way down\nthe lee of the Front Range. A cold front is expected to slide \nacross the forecast area later this afternoon and evening that \nwill shift surface winds to the northwest, advecting a much colder\nairmass into Colorado. \n\nWith colder temperatures expected behind the front, snow levels will \ndrop overnight, and we may see some snow flurries across the lower \nelevations by Saturday morning when temperatures will drop below \nfreezing, and QG forcing is greatest as the shortwave passes just to \nour south. Unfortunately, this once robust looking system just \nwon't have enough juice to bring any accumulating snowfall to our \nlower elevations, but we may see a few tenths in our high mountain\nvalleys, southern foothills, and along the Palmer Divide when all\nis said and done. Saturday's afternoon high temperatures will be \nroughly 5 to 10 degrees below seasonal values across the forecast \narea, with 30s and 40s forecasted for the plains, and 30s for the \nmountain valleys. \n\nBrief upper-level ridging is expected to return by Saturday night \nthat will bring drier conditions back to Colorado, but won't have \nmuch impact on the cooler temperatures. Afternoon high temps will \nfinally start to resemble November normals, with the plains actually \nlooking to be a few degrees below seasonal values through at least \nthe middle of next week. Uncertainties remain with the strength and \ntrack of our next potential shortwave (Monday?), but it's not\nlooking like any significant precipitation will be possible\noutside of a few waves of light mountain snowfall through the \nforecast period.",
